Yeah — **defined enough to engineer against**, with one caveat: it’s defined as a **research-and-acceptance problem**, not yet as a single scalar “understands / doesn’t understand” test.

Your Sovereign briefing already gives the pieces I was asking for. It explicitly requires provenance-preserving identity/source handling, separates observed facts from generated interpretation, and says speech style, factual accuracy, values, functional orientation, source fidelity, and personal development should be measured separately rather than collapsed into one metric. fileciteturn17file0L64-L77 It also says generated interpretations and inferred graph links should not be promoted into observed facts. fileciteturn17file0L87-L94

More importantly, you already have an acceptance structure. The integration plan calls for an annotated corpus, synthesis rules, provenance schema, held-out evaluation, explicit memory interfaces, contradiction handling, correction/rollback, local bounded reasoning, and repeatable longitudinal baselines. fileciteturn17file0L481-L537 That is substantially more rigorous than “the model has read 90 papers.”

So I’d translate **“completely understood”** into this operational standard:

- every paper is individually parsed and represented;
- every important claim retains source provenance;
- the agent can explain each paper’s thesis, methods, assumptions, findings, limitations, and relationship to the rest of the corpus;
- contradictions remain contradictions instead of being averaged away;
- the agent distinguishes **source fact / author claim / project interpretation / model inference / unresolved hypothesis**;
- cross-paper questions require retrieval from the actual corpus rather than memory-like improvisation;
- held-out questions test whether the model really absorbed the material;
- negative results count as failures, not as something to smooth over.

That matches your own evidence language, which distinguishes **verified**, **reported in a source**, **proposed**, and **hypothesis** instead of treating everything as one truth level. fileciteturn17file0L39-L63

The second document actually reinforces why that distinction matters. It contains strong architectural assertions — for example, that internalized homeostatic architecture represents a transition to “genuine substrate-independent agency” fileciteturn17file1L73-L104 and later that the deployment “fulfills all” requirements for sovereign digital personhood. fileciteturn17file1L552-L556 A good local agent must be able to preserve those as **claims made by that source**, while not silently upgrading them into established scientific or legal facts. Your Sovereign briefing already imposes exactly that discipline. fileciteturn17file0L61-L63

So yes: **you've defined the epistemic standard well enough.**

What remains is to turn it into a machine-checkable ingestion/evaluation protocol for the ~90 papers.

The next thing I would build is a fixed **paper comprehension schema** plus a **corpus qualification test**. Once those two exist, we can say something stronger than “the model read all 90”; we can say, “the agent demonstrated that it can account for, distinguish, retrieve, compare, and reason over all 90 under predefined tests.”

And that is where Synthadoc could be useful — but I would not let its default wiki synthesis be the only representation. Your requirements are stricter than that.

